==================================================================================================================
THREE-SENTENCE VERDICT
==================================================================================================================
Even granting the lead's integrable-clock action is healthy at galaxy scale (L71/L72's question, not
this lane's), it is bound by the excess-spent-once theorem exactly as every other single-metric branch
is: because ordinary matter is minimally coupled to ONE physical metric g, a cold component in the CMB's
amount -- which it still needs for clusters and the CMB -- transmits its Newtonian pull to baryons with
eta=1 in galaxies, overshooting the rotation curves by median x1.69, and keeping MOND inside the clock
changes WHERE the anomaly is produced (a different channel, which is how it escaped the L60/L69 kill) but
NOT the transmission factor eta, which is a property of the cold-matter->baryon channel the clock leaves
untouched.  The one route that could differ -- letting the clock sector supply cluster mass and structure
with no separate cold component -- does not escape either: a dust-like clock is a cold component that
inherits the same eta=1 overshoot, and a non-dust clock structure sector must reproduce the non-monotone
pair/cluster ladder (a LambdaCDM cold-halo signature) and the CMB while the lead's OWN mixed cosmology
(IC23) already loses causality at 0.4% of an e-fold, the same wall as the recorded g03w-g04j dark-sector
no-go.  So even the last candidate is complete only below a galaxy: 'a healthy version is still not a
complete theory' is the honest and load-bearing result, conditional on L71/L72; kappa=1/2 remains fitted
and nothing here favours any framework over LambdaCDM.

